{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>That you may enter.</b> Rabbi Abraham ibn Ezra explained: if you have begun to observe His commandments, and this is the first commandment, etc. And the Ramban explained to allude to the Torah, etc. But the verse does not sit well with either of the two interpretations. And there is a further point to raise in its saying <b>as HaShem, the Elohim of your fathers, spoke to you</b>, for there was no need to say the word ״לָךְ״ after it already said ״which you are entering the land as״ and so on — the matter is self-understood that the promise to them is what is intended." "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent of the verse is that since Moses had previously declared to Israel that their entry into the land was not on Israel's merit, as it says (Deuteronomy 9:5) ״not by your righteousness״ etc., but rather ״because of the wickedness of these nations״ etc. and ״in order to establish״ etc. — therefore his statement comes here: that through the performance of the deed described in the matter, their entry into the land will be on their own merit, and the decree of the verse is the word ״לָךְ״ stated at the end. And this is the measure of the verse: ״לְמַעַן אֲשֶׁר תָּבֹא״ — meaning, your entry into the land ״which HaShem your Elohim gives you, as… to you״ — meaning, so that you are worthy and will not need the merit of the covenant of the fathers. And understand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:19:18.410281599Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}